{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Package Diagrams in UML"}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear when talking about package diagram."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Build the macrosystem conceptual model following the customer requirements, through the use and interpretation of collected information, represented in class diagrams, interaction diagrams, and operation contracts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation.  Recognizes basic ideas on programming tools, diagrams of cases, and data flow diagrams."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modal Verb Can in Affirmative Statements and Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the modal verb <span class=\"strong\">can </span>to talk about possibilities."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use the modal verb <span class=\"strong\">can </span>in affirmative sentences to talk about something that is possible for someone to perform. The modal verb <span class=\"strong\">can </span>doesn't change, no matter what subject you use."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use the modal verb <span class=\"strong\">can </span>in affirmative present statement by following this structure:"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <div class=\"center_text\"> Subject + modal verb can + main verb + complement.</div>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">I <span class=\"strong\">can </span>create a package diagram.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">You can use the modal verb can in questions by following this structure.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<div class=\"center_text\"> Modal verb can +main verb+ subject + complement?</div>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Can </span>you create a package diagram?</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Can package diagrams show the architecture of a system?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Package diagrams, as maps, can show spatial information that represents the general architecture of a system."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Basic components, or packages, can help us arrange information in the diagram."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Drawing a Package Diagram Using the URL of an Organization"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">URL </span>for drawing unique package diagrams of an organization."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Organizations can create a unique URL. This URL is composed by the associations of several packages. Those packages highlight their relationship and determine a number of classes within each package."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Now, each part of the URL is a package. A package diagram shows part by part the composition of the URL. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Let's see how to convey this information into a package diagram: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<li> URL:  www.packagediagram.com</li> \t<li> Name: PackageSample</li> <li>#Packages: 3</li> <li>#Classes: 6</li>"}, {"type": "image", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09img01.jpg"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for a URL with package diagrams."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Describe the package diagrams that you can recognize."}, {"type": "text", "content": "List all the diagrams you see."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share it with your class."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for specific information in a URL."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I describe package diagrams with the information I learned in this learning material."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I list all the diagrams I can see in the URL. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "directory "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "group of files "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Not really, you can define package as a directory that contains a group of related classes."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "hierarchy "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the arrangement of things depending on how important they are "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can name information packages but remember that you have to follow a specific hierarchy."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "convey "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to express, to present"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can convey any kind of information but you always need to include a root directory of the package. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "root directory "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the first or most important directory in a hierarchy"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can convey any kind of information but you always need to include a root directory of the package and then the sub-directories of the system."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "depict "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to describe"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You can depict the classes or product information within the package."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "spatial "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that takes a specific space"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Sure! Package diagrams, as maps, can show spatial information that represents the general architecture of a system.y"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le01ob09aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n2le01ob09re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob09re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span>So, can package diagrams show the architecture of a system? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Designer:</span> Sure! Package diagrams, as maps, can show spatial information that represents the general architecture of a system. Basic components or packages can help us arrange information in the diagram. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span>Can you describe packages as a visual representation? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Designer:</span> Not really. You can define a package as a directory that contains a group of related classes. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span>Can I name each package the way I want? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Designer:</span>  Absolutely! You can name information packages, but remember that you have to follow a specific hierarchy, I mean, you can convey any kind of information, but you always need to include a root directory of the package and then the sub-directories of the system. <br/>"}, {"time": "47000", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob09re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span> Can you explain a package diagram to me? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Designer:</span>  Let me give you an example. You want your store to have a website, so you need the information you want to include. You can depict the classes or product information within the package; packages or type of clothing that contain products or classes and those dependencies you want to have between those dependencies. Once you have the information, you can create a diagram that represents packages and classes. <br/>"}, {"time": "75000", "image": "enpe8n2le01ob09re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span>How can I show that information to customers? <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Designer:</span> Well, you can use a unique URL for your organization; then you can include your three packages that represent target buyers; after that, you can show the classes or products you offer, and finally, include that information as part of one same catalogue. <br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Manager:</span>Great! My website can be www.clothes&clothes.com<br/>\n<span class=\"strong\">Designer:</span> Sure! But remember that you have to decode your URL once you create a package diagram."}], "coverimg": "enpe8n2le01ob09re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}]}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can simply say what a package in UML is. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can discuss simple ideas in real life that ex